; Somebody else's soldier. The same body the local player wears, on a physics
; body so it stops bullets, plus the two things only a remote soldier needs: a
; name over its head and a positional voice for its gunfire and its wounds.
;
; AnimatableBody3D rather than CharacterBody3D because nothing here simulates:
; the position arrives off the wire and is written straight in.
;
; It sits on layer 2 and collides with nothing, which is what keeps soldiers
; from shoving each other. That is not a preference -- a body whose position is
; teleported twenty times a second is not a body a physics engine can push
; against sanely, and two of them overlapping resolve the overlap by launching
; each other into the sky. Walking through somebody is a far smaller price than
; that, and layer 2 is still hit by every ray in the game: rounds, fragments and
; the tank's coaxial all query every layer, so a soldier standing there stops
; them exactly as before.
